+# Affine isomorphisms of split binary forms (arXiv:2511.06843)
+
+**Status: parked, not shippable yet.** The generator and all local gates G1–G8 pass, but the required external oracle run could not make one scored call: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” on every redraw. G9 and STEP 4 therefore remain unverified; `selftest_report.json` records this as a failure rather than treating API errors as model failures.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | polynomial identity |
+| Certificate | matrix certificate |
+| Intuition | invariant: centered root-moment covariance |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust boundary
+
+The family instantiates the Polynomial Isomorphism Search Problem defined in Section 8 of Martin Kreuzer’s [Code Equivalence, Point Set Equivalence, and Polynomial Isomorphism](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.06843). A panel gives expanded degree-(d) homogeneous binary forms (F,G\in\mathbb F_p[x,y]) and asks for an invertible matrix (A=[[1,u],[0,a]]) satisfying (F(A(x,y)^T)=G(x,y)). The checker substitutes the proposed matrix with exact modular binomial arithmetic and compares every coefficient. It never reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation samples (d) distinct roots (R\subset\mathbb F_p), expands (F=\prod_{r\in R}(x-r y)), samples (a\ne0,b), and expands (G=\prod_{r\in R}(x-(ar+b)y)). Consequently `[[1,-b],[0,a]]` is known by composition of identities before the instance exists. The roots are deliberately omitted from the rendered question.
+
+This is not a Track A claim. The paper’s Introduction notes structural attacks on special LCE regimes and extensive cryptanalysis of cubic IP1S; Section 10 says PI is believed efficiently solvable in the vast majority of cases. Here the explicit standard route factors each form over (mathbb F_p) and aligns its two root sets. General randomized finite-field factorization is polynomial in (d) and (log p); the elementary alignment used here is (O(d^3)). The measured standard-library specialization exhaustively evaluates all (p) field elements and used 3,584,262 field operations (0.124 s mean in G6) at the intended hard preset.
+
+The compact route uses Newton’s identities on only the first three coefficients. If (m,C_2,C_3) are the root mean and centered power sums, then (C'_2=a^2C_2), (C'_3=a^3C_3), and (m'=am+b). Thus (C_3/C_2) exposes the scale covariance and the means expose the translation. Ten independent panels require at most 280 exact field operations—short enough after the insight, but mechanically tedious without a calculator.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is `make_instance(seed=0, n=5, prime=17, panels=1)` in full:
+
+```text
+Polynomial Isomorphism Search over a prime field (batched).
+
+All arithmetic is in F_17: reduce every integer modulo 17.
+Each panel gives two homogeneous binary forms F(x,y), G(x,y) of degree 5.
+A displayed vector [c0,c1,...,cd] means exactly
+  c0*x^d + c1*x^(d-1)*y + ... + cd*y^d.
+Every displayed coefficient is the canonical integer in 0..p-1.
+
+For each panel, find a normalized affine change-of-variables matrix
+  A = [[1, u], [0, a]] with 0 <= u < p and 1 <= a < p
+such that F(A*[x,y]^T) = F(x+u*y, a*y) equals G(x,y)
+coefficient by coefficient in F_p.  The panels may be listed in any
+order, but your matrices must follow the displayed order.  Repetitions
+inside a matrix are allowed; no floating point or integer equality is intended.
+
+Panel 1
+F: [1,7,14,16,7,16]
+G: [1,3,9,2,8,11]
+
+Output one JSON object with exactly one 2x2 matrix per panel:
+{"matrices":[[[1,u1],[0,a1]],[[1,u2],[0,a2]],...]}.
+Use ordinary base-10 integers in 0..p-1 and no ellipses.
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as that exact JSON object.
+Example: <answer>{"matrices":[[[1,3],[0,5]]]}</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `{"matrices":[[[1,5],[0,9]]]}`. `verify(inst, answer)` returns `(True, "ok")`; changing the `5` to `6` returns `(False, "panel 0 coefficient mismatch at index 1")`. The demo is hand-solvable: it needs one 5-element panel over (mathbb F_{17}), so the centered-moment computation fits on paper.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | degree | prime | panels | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 5 | 17 | 1 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 40 | 1009 | 6 | oracle run attempted, but all calls errored |
+| medium | 60 | 1009 | 8 | not reached |
+| hard | 80 | 1009 | 10 | intended shipping preset; local gates measured here |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is provisionally `hard`; it is not an actual shipping claim until the bare and hinted harness runs return `hardened`.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset–seed witnesses verify; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | 7/7 corruptions rejected with 7 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged fenced JSON round-trips; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; language size (1.18445\times10^{60}) |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; demo exact count 1; reference 3,629,818 operations in 0.126 s |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks at 0/8; reference solves 8/8, 3,584,262 operations mean |
+| G7 | pass | degree 160 verifies with the same 40 answer atoms |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 | **blocked** | size caps pass (192 chars, 48 estimated tokens, 40 atoms, 280 operations); oracle polarity gate unmeasured |
+
+The G6 failures are raw coefficient ratio, translation-only greedy, 256 random restarts, scale-only ansatz, and an uncentered-moment ratio. The successful factor-and-align algorithm is correctly outside `attacks` because this is Track B.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9
+
+The harness-owned bare transcript contains only errors; errors do not consume attempts. Its four records are:
+
+| preset | seed | model | result |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| easy | 909246400 | Claude Sonnet 5 | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 2004244930 | Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 556052020 | Claude Sonnet 5 | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1253060276 | Claude Sonnet 5 | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / completed attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | blocked before scoring |
+| hinted | 0/0 | not run |
+| placebo | 0/0 | not run |
+
+Hinted minus placebo is undefined. No claim about the usefulness of the invariant hint is possible yet. After restoring OpenRouter quota, rerun bare in this directory, run hinted and placebo in separate scratch directories as prescribed, copy their harness-produced transcripts back, update `G9_RESULTS`, and regenerate `selftest_report.json`.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2511_06843 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=123, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after G9/STEP 4 complete successfully:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2511.06843 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This promise family is much narrower than unrestricted PI: the matrix is affine and normalized, every form splits into distinct linear factors over the base field, and instances with zero second or third centered moment are resampled. A tool-enabled solver should use the moment formula directly and will solve the family quickly; that is the point of Track B, not a weakness being hidden as Track A. The 0/200,000 density estimate is under independent uniform affine matrices for all ten panels; it does not estimate a solver prior that has found the centered-moment invariant, and batching makes random guessing look far worse than one panel’s roughly one-in-(p(p-1)) chance. No Gröbner-basis, tensor, or optimized Cantor–Zassenhaus implementation was benchmarked. Most importantly, no oracle hardness evidence exists until the external quota problem is fixed.
+
