codex
Implemented and locally verified a native Track B polynomial-isomorphism generator based on the paper’s Section 8 problem definition. The paper itself notes PI is believed efficiently solvable in most cases, so Track A would be unjustified. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2511.06843v2)

- [Generator module](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/gen_2511_06843.py)
- [Selftest report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/selftest_report.json) — all G1–G9 local gates pass
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/README.md)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)
- [Parking explanation](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2511.06843/PARKED.md)

Key measurements:

- Structure-aware guesses: 0/200,000
- Five adversarial attacks: 0/8 successes each
- Reference algorithm: solves 8/8, averaging 1,427,306 field operations
- Compact centered-moment route: at most 280 operations
- Answer: 86 characters, 16 atomic elements
- G8: 60/60 invariance and carried-witness checks

The result is parked, not rejected or claimed shipped. A fresh bare run had Google solve `easy`, but the required second vendor and both G9 arms were stopped by OpenRouter HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded.” Those errors remain recorded as errors and were not converted into hardness evidence.
